  "body": "As far as I'm aware of, there currently are two supported ways to change the playback position in a media element (e.g. ```<video>```):\r\n\r\n1. Using a link with [media fragments URI](https://www.w3.org/TR/media-frags/) like ```<a href=\"video.ogv#t=10\">Start playback at 00:00:10.000</a>```.\r\n  -> Doesn't work when video is already playing.\r\n2. By setting the ```currentTime``` attribute of the element.\r\n  -> needs javascript\r\n\r\n\r\nLike anchor links are used to \"seek\" to sections on an already displayed website, one could use \"extended\" anchor links to seek in an already playing media. They could just replicate setting currentTime (but without needing javascript).\r\nIt would be nice to have if other methods could be replicated aswell.\r\nNaively, I believe this should be easy to implement by just parsing the URI and forward parameters to the already present API used by javascript (i'm probably very wrong on this one).\r\n\r\n# Possible use cases\r\nConsidering some video element:\r\n```\r\n<video id=\"myVideo\">\r\n  <source ...>\r\n</video>\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Seek to position\r\nThis could be a javascriptless chapter link:\r\n```\r\n<a href=\"#myVideo&t=5.5\">Seek to 00:00:05.500</a>\r\n```\r\nit would replicate this:\r\n```\r\n<a href=\"#\" onclick=\"seekTo(5.5)\">Seek to 00:00:05.500</a>\r\n\r\n<script>\r\n  function seekTo(t) {\r\n    var video = document.getElementById(\"myVideo\");\r\n    video.currentTime = t;\r\n  }\r\n</script>\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Seek to position and start playback\r\nSeek to position and start playback.\r\n(This could impose trouble with absolute or external links and autoplay restrictions but otoh it _should_ already be solved in the javascript API. Browser developers would know better about that than I am):\r\n```\r\n<a href=\"#myVideo&t=5.5&play=true>\r\n```\r\nwould replicate this javascript:\r\n```\r\nvar video = document.getElementById(\"myVideo\");\r\nvideo.currentTime = 5.5;\r\nvideo.play();\r\n```\r\n\r\n----\r\nThe above shouldn't serve as possible solution to seek in media elements without javascript but rather serve as example. My key thought is \"Anchor links are already working. Why not use them for media elements aswell?\"",
